WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.410
On September 15th 1940 the Luftwaffe began
a voracious campaign, targeting London.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:11.380
A sudden change in tactics, focusing the full
might of their air force on the most heavily

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.530
populated city in Great Britain.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:20.439
Over the course of this brutal day, over 1100
German aircraft entered British airspace.

00:00:20.439 --> 00:00:26.890
An astonishing invasion to attack a non-strategic
civilian target, however this was all part

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:31.519
of the Luftwaffe’s plan to gain air superiority
in British skies.

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:36.640
Baiting British fighter planes into the sky
to face off against their German counterparts.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.160
A showdown to eliminate the royal air force
as a threat, to pave the way for a German

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.629
amphibious invasion of Britain.

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:50.910
On this first day of the infamous Blitz over
1000 aircraft on both sides tangled in the

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.070
skies above London.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:59.030
The Luftwaffe believed the RAF were on their
last legs, but that couldn’t have been further

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.480
from the truth.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.810
The Spitfires and Hurricanes, being manufactured
in astonishing numbers, out pacing the attrition

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.939
of the battle, rose to meet the challenge.

00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:13.860
The pilots of the Luftwaffe, flying the BF
109, were astonished at the resistance they

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.860
met.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.360
However, another number stands out to me from
this period.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.400
Over the course of this month, the 6th anti-aircraft
division, covering the Thames estuary, the

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.220
main approach to London from Europe, expended
over a quarter of a million anti-aircraft

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.820
rounds in its effort to protect the citizens
of London.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:41.200
A bewildering 1800 rounds were needed to take
down a single aircraft on this day, according

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.229
to official records.

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:50.610
These numbers are largely accepted by experts
as false, with actual rounds needed much higher.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:56.770
Many downed German aircraft claimed by anti-aircraft
crews were in fact shot down by fighters in

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.000
the sky.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.140
The cause of these false reports and anti-aircrafts
technologies' ineffectiveness were the same.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.690
So, why was anti-aircraft technology during
the Battle of Britain so ineffective at damaging

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.290
invading planes?

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:16.920
And how did an ingenious, top secret technology,
introduced in the middle of the war change

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.110
the face of the battlefield forever?

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:28.360
At 
the outbreak of the war anti-aircraft technology

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.570
was, for the most part, a deterrent.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:36.450
A weapon that became vastly more effective
at lower altitudes, and in turn, forced enemy

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:42.050
aircraft high above the clouds where they
themselves struggled to hit the mark.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:47.019
The task of accurately targeting a high flying
aircraft from the ground was an even more

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:48.690
immense challenge.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:54.269
In the best case scenario anti-aircraft crews
had a direct line of sight with their target,

00:02:54.269 --> 00:02:58.020
allowing them to estimate both altitude, bearing,
and speed.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:02.580
This information was used to aim their barrels
to a location in front of the formation's

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.020
current location.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.560
An educated guess of where they would be when
the shells arrived.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.440
To make this task even harder, allied fuses
had to be timed on the ground.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.160
Turning a dial on a mechanically timed fuze.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.970
Calculating the time it would take for the
round to reach the target in the heat of battle

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.430
was difficult.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:27.409
These calculations needed to be incredibly
accurate, allowing the anti-aircraft rounds

00:03:27.409 --> 00:03:31.209
to explode in close proximity to their target.

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:35.819
This variable was also subject to change as
pilots were also instructed to change their

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:42.660
altitude and bearing randomly to make it impossible
to predict flight time and future location.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:48.360
This was a game of chance, and as such, relied
on an incredible volume of fire to stand a

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.970
chance of damaging their target.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:55.700
This problem needed to be solved, and was
high on the mind of the British as they held

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.270
through the brutal storm of this bombing campaign.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:04.459
For anti-aircraft rounds to become vastly
more effective they needed a way to detect

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:05.950
their target.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:11.099
In the era of fragile vacuum tubes, building
an electronic device capable of surviving

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:16.799
the immense 20,000 g acceleration launch out
of a gun barrel was a task that was deemed

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:18.290
impossible.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:24.070
Work began on radar triggered fuzes in both
Britain and Germany before the war broke out,

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:29.639
and both abandoned the idea completely for
gun launched projectiles, seeing no way to

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:34.169
overcome the technical challenge of ruggedizing
the electronics, while miniaturizing them

00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:37.640
to fit into a small artillery shell fuze.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.570
However, the Americans persevered and came
up with this.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.050
The VT Fuze.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:48.760
With its ominous red vial of liquid at its
core serving a vital purpose.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:53.060
The challenge of finding a suitable power
source for this radar transmitter was another

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:55.650
challenge facing the engineers.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:01.169
Early versions of the proximity fuze utilized
existing dry cell battery technology, but

00:05:01.169 --> 00:05:03.580
these came with 2 huge issues.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:09.530
Their charge would fade in just 2-3 months,
making long-term storage of these fuzes impossible.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.960
[1]
And secondly, the risk of having power accidentally

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.350
connect to the transmitter and receiver was
far too high.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:21.860
A single failure point could explode an entire
ammunition stockpile.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:26.870
For a reminder of what that looks like, this
is not a nuclear bomb.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:31.960
This is the SS John Burke, an United States
ammunition transport ship that was struck

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:38.160
by a Japanese kamikaze bomber while transporting
ammunition to the Philippines in 1944.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.540
The war in the pacific was the driving force
for the invention of the proximity fuze.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:49.060
The US were losing an astonishing number of
ships to Japanese aerial attacks and desperately

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:54.090
needed a better way to defend their ships
with anti-aircraft fire.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:59.160
The solution the US National Defence Research
Committee came up with to solve this issue

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:04.880
of safely powering this device was as bizarre
and unique as it was ingenious.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.849
The engineers switched to a wet cell design.

00:06:07.849 --> 00:06:11.740
This red vial contains the electrolyte of
the battery.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.310
This allowed for an ingenious work-around
for both problems.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:20.080
The liquid electrolyte was stored inside the
battery, separated from the solid plates of

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.440
the battery inside this vial.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.530
Underneath this glass ampule lay a breaker
that contained a clever mechanism.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.530
Two levers were pushed towards the center
of the mechanism by a spring.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:36.539
[2] When the round began to spin at 50,000
revolutions per minute, as a result of the

00:06:36.539 --> 00:06:42.070
rifling in the gun barrel, these levers would
be forced out, which released a trigger, allowing

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:47.289
a small explosive primer to force a spiked
plunger to break free and strike the glass

00:06:47.289 --> 00:06:48.360
ampule.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.599
Breaking it and releasing the electrolyte
into the battery.

00:06:51.599 --> 00:06:57.300
The plates of the battery were composed of
thin zinc plates coated in carbon on one side.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:02.160
Each layer separated by a plastic insulating
ring and each disk had holes punched into

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.090
it to allow the electrolyte to flow and fill
each layer.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:11.199
This glass could ofcourse break with rough
handling, but the electrolyte would not fill

00:07:11.199 --> 00:07:16.370
the space between the battery plates without
the centrifugal force of the round forcing

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.949
the liquid outwards into the surrounding plates.

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:24.770
Other safety measures were incorporated to
make absolutely sure that the chances of an

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:30.760
accidental detonation could only happen when
the round was in the air and spun at a tremendous

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:31.760
speed.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:32.760
[3]

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.200
Once energized this battery would provide
power to the oscillator, which generated a

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:40.620
continuous sine wave radio current to the
transmitter.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:46.139
For space efficiency's sake, the antenna operated
as both a transmitter and receiver.

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:50.560
With the currents traveling through the same
antenna, the fuze needed a way to differentiate

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.930
between the signals sent and signal received.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:57.199
For this the engineers could turn to the doppler
effect.

00:07:57.199 --> 00:08:01.919
As the target and fuze are traveling towards
each other we can easily calculate the apparent

00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:05.050
frequency shift due to their relative velocity.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:10.069
Then, with some basic math, we can come up
with this equation by subtracting the reflected

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:13.090
frequency from the actual frequency.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.270
This was called the beat note.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:18.710
This beat note is what the fuze uses to detonate
the round.

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:23.849
The amplitude of this beat signal will also
grow as the round approaches the target.

00:08:23.849 --> 00:08:27.789
Providing a signal that looks something like
this with amplification. [4]

00:08:27.789 --> 00:08:32.990
The fuze was tuned to explode at the optimum
distance and angle away from the target by

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:38.440
tailoring the radar sensitivity to match the
fragmentation pattern of these shells.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:42.940
When the round approached the target, the
returning radar waves would generate the necessary

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:48.529
threshold voltage to trigger the thyratron,
a type of high speed electrical switch.

00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:54.200
It looks a lot like a vacuum tube, but it
is filled with a low pressure gas like hydrogen.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.450
When a voltage of sufficient magnitude was
passed from the amplifier to the cathode grid

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:05.170
of the thyratron it would ionize the gas inside
and allow current to flow to the anode through

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:08.440
its glowing cloud of ionized atoms.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:13.820
Completing a circuit to a capacitor that would
in turn detonate the high explosive charge

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:15.130
in the round.

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:19.880
Creating a cloud of shrapnel at the optimum
distance way from the target.

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:25.000
This whole assembly, consisting of the vividly
red electrolyte capsule, the zinc-carbon battery

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:30.730
plates, oscillator, antenna, amplified and
thyratron was then inserted into the fuze

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:36.780
body and the cavities of the fuze were filled
with a microcrystalline wax for moisture protection,

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:42.820
and critically, mechanical support the electronics
to keep everything in place during launch.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:47.630
Packaging this all in a fuse this small in
the 1940s was no easy feat.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:53.519
The tubes manufactured for the proximity fuze
were drastically smaller than existing conventional

00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:54.760
tubes.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:10:01.089
This of course helped immensely with resisting
the force of launch, with 20,000 gs of acceleration

00:10:01.089 --> 00:10:04.540
every extra gram of weight saving makes the
difference.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:09.890
With this multiplication of mass, a single
gram would weigh 20 kilograms.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:14.920
The engineering challenge of ensuring every
gram of mass inside this fuze was supported

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:19.029
by a structure capable of bearing 20 kilograms
was immense.

00:10:19.029 --> 00:10:24.740
A challenge that deterred both the British
and Germans, and its success drastically changed

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:27.050
the face of the battlefield.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:31.190
The impact this fuze had on the war cannot
be overstated.

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:36.800
The atomic bomb was the punctuation mark of
this world wide war, but the statistics back

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.029
the VT Fuze as THE most impactful invention
of the war.

00:10:41.029 --> 00:10:44.290
An invention that changed the narrative of
the war completely.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:49.730
A counter to the newly invented airplane,
causing havoc across every theater of the

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:50.940
war.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.960
Such importance was placed on this invention
that it was classified as top secret and only

00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:01.500
approved for use in the pacific, where any
unexploded rounds would be lost to sea.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:06.890
[REF] Much of the manufacturing was performed
in smaller subassemblies to minimize any knowledgeable

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:10.570
manufacturer from deducing the purpose of
the device.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.220
And for good reason.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:17.680
During the London blitz its estimated that
1800 rounds were needed to take down a single

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:23.370
aircraft, but with the introduction of teh
VT fuze this number lowered to an average

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.360
of 85-100 rounds.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:28.769
A 10-20 times increase in lethality.

00:11:28.769 --> 00:11:34.769
A jump in lethality that great is akin to
the introduction of the machine gun.

00:11:34.769 --> 00:11:39.279
This was, beyond a doubt, a game changing
invention.

00:11:39.279 --> 00:11:44.800
At the peak of their production over 250,000
of these fuzes were being manufactured a week.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:52.360
The VT fuze was first fired on January 4th,
1943 on board the cruiser Helena, defending

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:57.029
itself from an aerial assault and successfully
shooting the aircraft down.

00:11:57.029 --> 00:12:01.500
This device had a devastating effect on the
aerial battles of the pacific.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:07.940
Japan soon began to run low on trained pilots
and a year later began the Kamikaze campaign,

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:12.960
sending waves of untrained pilots towards
American ships in the hopes just one could

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.269
get through.

00:12:14.269 --> 00:12:22.839
In May 1945 two destroyers off Okinawa found
themselves the targets of 150 kamikaze aircraft.

00:12:22.839 --> 00:12:24.870
Every single one of them was shot down.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:30.089
A feat that could not have been achieved without
the radio proximity fuze.

00:12:30.089 --> 00:12:36.089
Allowing the guns of these boats to fire relentlessly
with accurate fire with no pauses to fiddle

00:12:36.089 --> 00:12:38.389
with mechanical timed fuzes.

00:12:38.389 --> 00:12:43.720
Had it been available during the Battle of
Britain the battle would have been even shorter

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:49.550
lived with the anti-aircraft guns of London
suddenly becoming an order of magnitude more

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:54.240
effective the swarms of Luftwaffe bombers
would have stood little chance of returning

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.240
home.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:59.940
It was finally approved for use over land
for the first time in conjunction with the

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:05.180
Allied invasion of Normandy, a week before
Germany began bombarding Britain with the

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:06.440
V1 Buzz Bomb.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:12.100
At its peak 100 V1s were launched towards
Britain, and with the help of the VT Fuze

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:16.070
80% of these automated missiles were shot
down.

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:20.740
The VT Fuze also played a pivotal role in
the battle of the bulge, allowing artillery

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:26.120
fire to explode at the perfect distance overhead,
showering the canopy of the ardennes forest

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:27.730
with deadly shrapnel.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:32.420
Repelling Germany's final large scale counter
offensive of the war.

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:37.380
Even at this stage of the war, with Germany
on the retreat, the VT fuze was a closely

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:39.050
guarded secret.

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:42.520
Each and every fuze needed to be tracked and
accounted for.

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:47.600
With one story of artillery officers spending
hours searching through snow to locate lost

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:54.089
fuzes during the battle of the bulge, only
for them to be returned to them by locals.

00:13:54.089 --> 00:13:57.360
World War 2 was defined by technological progress.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:02.480
When the entire world was at war and nations'
very survival was at stake, innovation was

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:08.500
placed at the very pinnacle of national interests,
and this is the reason I find the war so fascinating.

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:10.740
I have created two series.

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:15.399
The logistics of d-day and the Battle of Britain
detail the innovations that helped win these

00:14:15.399 --> 00:14:16.730
critical battles.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:21.790
I put little focus on the military tactics
or individual soldiers on the ground.

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:26.570
This is the view of these battles from the
perspective of an engineering nerd.

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:30.810
The logistics of transporting tanks across
the english channel, how the giant game like

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:35.221
board at the center of RAF command worked
during the battle of Britain, and the well

00:14:35.221 --> 00:14:39.720
the final episode of the Battle of Britain
shares the astonishing manufacturing innovations

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:45.209
that allowed Britain to out manufacture Germany
in fighter planes and prevent aircraft attrition

00:14:45.209 --> 00:14:47.140
from effecting their ranks.

